PART 2: Colossal Cave Adventure
===============================

The files adventure/engine.py and adventure/data.py, and the tools that produced and
checked them (tools/gen_adventure_data.py, tools/crosscheck_adventure.py), are a
Python version of COLOSSAL CAVE ADVENTURE, the game by Will Crowther and Don Woods.
They are derived from troglobit/adventure (https://github.com/troglobit/adventure,
Joachim Wiberg), a cleaned-up Unix/Linux version of the BDS C conversion of the game.
The game text, the map, the vocabulary and the rules are theirs, and are unchanged.

Licence position
----------------
The C source carries no copyright notices in its files. The repository it comes from
contains a LICENSE file with the Unlicense, a public domain dedication:

    This is free and unencumbered software released into the public domain.
    Anyone is free to copy, modify, publish, use, compile, sell, or distribute this
    software, either in source code form or as a compiled binary, for any purpose,
    commercial or non-commercial, and by any means.

and debian/copyright in that repository lists the Unlicense for all files, naming as
copyright holders William Crowther (1975-1977), Don Woods (1977), Jay R. Jaeger
(1981), Jerry D. Pohl (1990) and James Lummel (1993). That dedication was made by
the maintainers of the Unix port. The repository's README, however, says of the
original game:

    "The actual license of this program is unknown, although most people agree that
    it is likely to be considered to be under the public domain."

So: the Unix port is dedicated to the public domain, and the game as a whole is widely
treated as public domain, but nobody has seen a licence from its original authors.
Unlike the Dungeon files above there is no restriction on commercial use. If you are
a rights holder and want something changed or removed, please open an issue.

Credits (from doc/AUTHORS and debian/copyright of the C port)
-------------------------------------------------------------
* Will Crowther: original game for the PDP-10 (Fortran, 1975-77). Willie Crowther
  in the C port's AUTHORS file.
* Don Woods: major features added at the Stanford AI Lab, 1977.
* J. R. Jaeger: conversion to BDS C for Intel 8080 and Zilog Z80 systems (CP/M), 1981.
* Jerry D. Pohl: UNIX standardisation, 1990.
* James Lummel: port to QNX 4, with various bug fixes, 1993.
* Joachim Wiberg: port to Linux with various cleanup (troglobit/adventure).
* This Python version: a hand-written port of the C logic, with the data (text, map,
  vocabulary, starting state) extracted from the C headers by
  tools/gen_adventure_data.py and the whole checked against the C program by
  tools/crosscheck_adventure.py (see README).

Changes made in this version
----------------------------
* The game logic is ported function by function and its behaviour is meant to be
  identical; it was compared with the C program on hundreds of thousands of commands.
* Four defects of the C source are fixed, because each one ends or corrupts a game
  (details at the top of adventure/engine.py):
  1. room 140 (the maze "Dead end" with the vending machine) could be entered but
     not left: the C code printed "Fatal error number 42" at the next move, and its
     arrays had no entry for the room;
  2. "back" was fatal after many moves (for instance "d" through the grate, then
     "back"), because it asked for the travel table of exits that lead to a message
     rather than a room;
  3. "back" also used a room number as an index into a 17-entry array;
  4. "look" next to a dwarf left the game waiting for a command at location 0.
  Typing "log" followed by an object still ends the game with "Fatal error number
  39", as in the C game (the verb has no message); here that closes the session and
  keeps the save.
* The start-up question "Would you like instructions?" is skipped (the game starts
  as if the answer was no, which gives the lamp 330 turns).
* The in-game "suspend"/"save" and "restore"/"resume" commands, and the save file in
  the user's home directory, are gone: the host (MeshToys) saves each player's game
  automatically after every command. Nothing in the game touches files or the
  environment.
* The random numbers come from a small generator whose state is part of the saved
  game (the C program always seeded rand() with 511).

"Colossal Cave Adventure" is the name of the game by Crowther and Woods. MeshToys is
not affiliated with or endorsed by any of the people named above.


PART 3: Hunt the Wumpus
=======================

The wumpus/ package implements the rules of Gregory Yob's 1973 game Hunt the Wumpus
(cave map, hazards, arrow and Wumpus behaviour). The rules were taken from the BASIC
listing in github.com/RayKV423/original-hunt-the-wumpus (marked Unlicense there), used
as a specification only. The code and all messages are new; no BASIC code or text was
copied, so the files here are covered by MeshToys's own licence.
